%N A049463 Number of basic interval orders of length n.
%C A049463 One may represent a basic length n interval order using n distinct endpoints. The removal of any element from such an order yields an interval order with shorter length.
%C A049463 See the Wikipedia article for the definition of interval order.
%D A049463 Amy N. Myers, Basic Interval Orders, Order, Volume: 16, Issue: 3, 1999, pp. 261-275.
%H A049463 Alois P. Heinz, <a href="/A049463/b049463.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 2..200</a>
%H A049463 Sean A. Irvine, <a href="https://github.com/archmageirvine/joeis/blob/master/src/irvine/oeis/a049/A049463.java">Java program</a> (github)
%H A049463 Amy N. Myers, <a href="https://www.brynmawr.edu/people/amy-n-myers">Home page</a> at Bryn Mawr College.
%H A049463 Amy N. Myers, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1006477416166">Basic Interval Orders</a>, Order, Volume: 16, Issue: 3, 1999, pp. 261-275. [Paywall]
%H A049463 Amy N. Myers, <a href="https://collections.dartmouth.edu/archive/object/dcdis/dcdis-myers1999">Results in Enumeration and Topology of Interval Orders</a>, Ph.D. Thesis at Dartmouth College.
%H A049463 David Radcliffe, <a href="https://gist.github.com/Radcliffe/a56187c144317e1f9ae31ebbf975fb90">Python script for sequence A049463</a>
%H A049463 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interval_order">Interval order</a>
%F A049463 a(2) = 1. For n >= 3, a(n) = Sum_{k=1..n-2} Sum_{l=k..n-2, l!=k+1} o(n-1,k,l)*(2^k + n - l - 2), where o(n,k,l) = 0 if min(n,k,l) < 1; o(n,k,l) = 1 if k = l = 1; and otherwise o(n,k,l) = (n-l)*o(n-1,k-1,l-1) + k*o(n-1,k,l) + Sum_{j=k+1..l-1, j!=l-2} binomial(j,k-1)*o(n-1,j,l-1). Formula due to Myers. - _David Radcliffe_, Sep 24 2026
%e A049463 a(2)=1 since {[ 1,1 ],[ 2,2 ]} is the unique basic interval order with two distinct endpoints.
